\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 2. Dynamic tool gating\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Picking up the problem left open last session.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 A menu that reprints itself before you sit down. A VIP member sees the full menu, a regular guest sees a shorter one \'97 not because they're asked not to order the VIP items, but because those items aren't printed at all.\
With 
\f2\fs26 standard_booking
\f0\fs24  and 
\f2\fs26 vip_lounge_booking
\f0\fs24  both bound, a regular user asking for a VIP seat gets one. Two bad workarounds: asking the user whether they're a VIP (everyone says yes), or removing the tool entirely (then real VIPs can't use it).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 With dynamic tools, the set of tools available to the agent is modified at runtime rather than defined all up front.\
Two approaches: 
\f1\b filter pre-registered tools
\f0\b0  when everything is known at creation time, or load tools during the run (MCP discovery). Filtering is what this session covers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Middleware, in brief\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Middleware lets you cut into the loop at defined points: 
\f2\fs26 before_agent
\f0\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 before_model
\f0\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 wrap_model_call
\f0\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 wrap_tool_call
\f0\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 after_model
\f0\fs24 , 
\f2\fs26 after_agent
\f0\fs24 . Before calling a tool you can authenticate; after it you can log or scrub. One student compared it to advice in Spring AI, which fits.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 @wrap_model_call\
def gate_vip_tools(request, handler):\
    is_vip = request.state.get("is_vip_member", False)\
    if not is_vip:\
        allowed = [t for t in request.tools if t.name != "vip_lounge_booking"]\
        request = request.override(tools=allowed)\
    return handler(request)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 Middleware acts as a filter between the agent and its tools. VIP false 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  one tool passes. VIP true 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  both pass.\
The documented variants shown: state-based tool filtering (authenticated users get sensitive tools, unauthenticated get only 
\f2\fs26 public_*
\f0\fs24  tools; also gating advanced tools until message count exceeds a threshold) and store-based filtering off feature flags keyed by user ID.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 The bug that took a break to fix\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 The first live attempt failed: passing 
\f2\fs26 is_vip_member=True
\f0\fs24  had no effect and the model kept saying it had no VIP tools. Cause: 
\f1\b the agent didn't know that field existed.
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs26 \cf0 class TripState(AgentState):\
    is_vip_member: bool = False\
\
agent = create_agent(..., state_schema=TripState)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf0 You have to declare the schema. Only then does the value land in state where middleware and tools can read it. Same idea as ChatGPT tracking 
\f2\fs26 is_pro_user
\f0\fs24 .\
Why not a plain Python 
\f2\fs26 if/else
\f0\fs24  outside the agent? Your Python code can't read the agent's state or store, and it can't react as the message count grows. It sees message count 0 forever.\
Real-world framing: Prime members getting faster refunds from a support agent is this pattern.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 3. Headless tools\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Some tools cannot run on your server. Clipboard access, geolocation, latency checks, payments \'97 these run where the user's app runs, usually the browser.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Headless tools are tool definitions \'97 name, description, argument schema \'97 that you register on the server with your agent. The implementation is registered only on the client and executed after a short interrupt-and-resume handshake.\
Three tiers to keep straight:\

